---
// The redaction tool page, HELD (#136, slice S1).
//
// NOT SHIPPED, and not built into a production site. This file lives outside `src/pages/`, so
// Astro does not route it; `astro.config.mjs` injects `/redact-pdf` only when
// `BURROW_HARNESS=1`, exactly as it does `/harness`. `src/production-build.test.ts` asserts the
// route is absent from a production build and present in the harness one, because "we intended
// not to ship it" is a different claim from "it is not there". It comes off the held list when
// the ship blockers in `docs/ROADMAP.md` close, deliberately, not because this page exists.
//
// THE COPY IS ADR 0029 §7's, and its sentences come from `src/redact-copy.ts` so
// `src/redact-copy.test.ts` can hold the built page to them: every mark present, "burrow"
// absent from the visible prose, and none of the forbidden claims made. Write "Not Only PDF",
// never "burrow" -- the repository is burrow; the product a person is using is not.
//
// THE ISLAND (S3) mounts into `#redact-tool` by hand, as every tool page's does: a file, every
// page's frame, one region typed in points, a run through redaction's own worker, and a download.
// Pictures and pointer selection are S4.
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import {
  ALT_TEXT_MARK,
  ALT_TEXT_UNSEEN_MARK,
  FONT_SET_MARK,
  FONT_SHARED_MARK,
  NOT_CHANGED_MARK,
  NOT_CHANGED_WHY_MARK,
  ORIGINAL_MARK,
  REFUSES_MARK,
  REMOVED_MARK,
  SCAN_MARK,
  SCAN_NOT_A_LINE_MARK,
  SCAN_REFUSED_MARK,
  VERIFIES_LIMIT_MARK,
  VERIFIES_MARK,
  WHERE_TO_LOOK_MARK,
} from "../redact-copy.js";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Redact PDF — remove text on your own computer | Not Only PDF"
  description="Remove selected text from a PDF. The redaction happens in your browser and the file is never uploaded, and the page says plainly what it removes and what it does not."
>
  <div class="read">
    <h1>Redact PDF</h1>

    <p>
      Remove text you select from a PDF. The work happens in this browser, on this computer: the
      file is not uploaded, because Not Only PDF has no server that could receive it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div id="redact-tool"></div>

  <script>
    import { type Component, mount } from "svelte";

    import RedactTool from "../components/RedactTool.svelte";

    const target = document.getElementById("redact-tool");
    if (target) {
      // Astro types a `.svelte` default export as an Astro component; mounting it by hand is the
      // same module reached through Svelte's own API (see `rotate-pdf.astro`).
      mount(RedactTool as unknown as Component, { target });
    }
  </script>

  <noscript>
    <div class="read no-js">
      <h2>This tool needs JavaScript</h2>
      <p>
        The redacting is done by code running in your browser, so with JavaScript switched off there
        is nothing to do the work. There is no server-side version to fall back to.
      </p>
    </div>
  </noscript>

  {
    /*
    OPEN BY DEFAULT, as `/split-pdf`'s is and for the same reason: what is inside is the
    explanation of what the operation leaves behind, and a disclosure a person has to click to
    find is weaker than the one ADR 0029 §7 describes.
  */
  }
  <div class="read">
    <details open>
      <summary><h2>What is removed, and what is not</h2></summary>

      <p>
        {REMOVED_MARK} Anything the page itself carried about that text — its thumbnail, its accessibility
        text, the private notes some editors attach to a page — goes with it.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>{NOT_CHANGED_MARK}</strong>
        {NOT_CHANGED_WHY_MARK} — so if the text you removed also appears in the title, in an attached
        file, or in the properties your editor wrote when it saved, it is still in this file.
        <strong>{WHERE_TO_LOOK_MARK}</strong> If either holds what you were removing, the fix is in the
        program you made the document with, not here.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>{ALT_TEXT_MARK}</strong> A tagged document can carry a written-out version of what a passage
        says, which a screen reader reads <em>instead of</em> the letters on the page. That version covers
        the whole passage as one piece of writing, with nothing in it to say which words belong to the
        part you removed — so Not Only PDF removes the whole thing rather than guessing at a shorter one.
        A screen reader then falls back to the letters on the page. Usually those are fine. But the reason
        an author supplies that text in the first place is often that the letters do <strong
          >not</strong
        >
        read correctly on their own — a ligature that is one character in the file and two in the word,
        or a word broken across two lines — and in those documents the fallback is worse than what was
        there.
        <strong>{ALT_TEXT_UNSEEN_MARK}</strong>, and the person it affects is not the person doing
        the redacting, which is why it is written here rather than left to be discovered.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>{FONT_SET_MARK}</strong> Not the words, and not the order — the set of characters. Rebuilding
        a font is a thing Not Only PDF cannot do yet, so this is stated rather than fixed.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>{FONT_SHARED_MARK}</strong> Not the words, and not where they were — but more than the
        character set, because the <code>/Widths</code> array and the <code>/ToUnicode</code> map still
        have an entry per code. Not Only PDF leaves that font untouched on purpose: editing it would change
        how the text looks on the other pages that use it, which is damage to a page you did not ask about
        rather than a leak on the one you did.
      </p>

      <p>
        {REFUSES_MARK}
        <strong>And {VERIFIES_MARK}:</strong>
        {VERIFIES_LIMIT_MARK}.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>{ORIGINAL_MARK}</strong> Not Only PDF makes a new file and leaves the one you chose as
        it was, so the original still holds everything you removed. Keep it or delete it as you would
        any other copy of what you were protecting.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details open>
      <summary><h2>Scanned pages</h2></summary>

      <p>
        A scanned page is usually one picture of the whole page, so the letters you can see are part
        of the picture rather than text in the file. {SCAN_REFUSED_MARK} Taking those letters out would
        mean darkening the pixels inside the area you chose and saving the picture again, and
        {SCAN_MARK}. {SCAN_NOT_A_LINE_MARK} — until it does, a scanned page is refused rather than handed
        back looking redacted.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
